    chapter: "Chapter 1 — The Receipt That Wouldn't Burn",
    text: `Dana Rowe arrived at the Harbor Industrial warehouse while the roof was still breathing smoke, a gray plume leaning inland under a wind that smelled of wet asphalt and scorched plastic. Engine Three had knocked the main body down an hour earlier. What remained was the patient work of overhaul: pry bars, thermal cameras, the careful collapse of beams that looked solid until they weren't. Dana signed the scene log, pulled on gloves still warm from the previous night's call, and stepped through a doorway whose frame had blistered into charcoal lace.

She had been a fire investigator for eleven years. Warehouse fires usually told the same story in different accents — overloaded breaker panels, careless welding, sometimes an insurance policy that had grown suspiciously large in the months before the match. This one already felt wrong in the way a room feels wrong when someone has rearranged the furniture while you were gone. The burn pattern on the west wall rose in a narrow, hungry column inconsistent with the stacked cardboard the owner claimed had been the only fuel. Accelerant dogs had alerted twice near a floor drain that should have been empty.

Near the drain, half-buried under a collapsed rack of shipping crates, Dana found a paper receipt.

It should not have survived. Thermal paper blackens and curls at temperatures far below what this room had endured. Yet the slip lay pale against the ash, edges crisp, ink still legible in places that made no chemical sense. She photographed it in situ, then lifted it with tweezers into an evidence sleeve. The merchant line read MERIDIAN CHEMICAL SUPPLY — RESTORATION DIVISION. The total was forty-seven dollars and twelve cents. The timestamp was three nights earlier.

When she angled the sleeve toward her flashlight, secondary writing bloomed through the ash that clung to the paper: names, thin as hairline fractures, written in a gray that seemed to drink the light rather than reflect it.

LILA MOREAU. THEO BRANDT. NORA KIM.

Dana stood very still in the ruined aisle, listening to water drip from a broken sprinkler head, and understood — with the particular cold clarity her job had taught her to trust — that someone had wanted this found.`,
