    chapter: "Chapter 2 — Names in Ash",
    text: `Theo Brandt agreed to meet Dana at a diner near City Hall because he did not trust conference rooms where conversations acquired minutes and witnesses. He was fifty-four, thin in the way of men who skipped meals for inspections, and he stirred sugar into coffee he did not drink while Dana showed him a cropped photograph of the ash names.

"I signed Harbor Industrial's sprinkler variances," he said finally. "Three times. Conrad Voss's attorney sat in every meeting with a smile that never reached his eyes and a binder full of temporary remediation plans that somehow became permanent. I flagged the west-wall drainage as inadequate two years ago. The variance still went through."

"Why keep signing?"

Brandt's mouth twisted. "Because the board of appeals overrode me, and because Northbridge Mutual's adjuster kept producing letters saying the risk profile was acceptable pending upgrades that never quite arrived. Paige Ellery wrote half those letters. I kept copies." He slid a USB drive across the table. "Everything I was afraid to put in the official file. If something happens to me, that drive goes to Nora Kim at the Herald. She already knows part of it."

Dana pocketed the drive. "Someone left your name on a receipt that survived a warehouse fire. Lila Moreau is dead. I need you to stay somewhere that isn't your house tonight."

"I have a daughter in Cleveland," Brandt said. "I'm not running to Cleveland because a piece of paper got poetic." He hesitated. "There is one more thing. Six months ago, a man claiming to be a private investigator asked me about Meridian Chemical deliveries to Voss properties. He knew details he shouldn't have. Called himself Merritt."

Dana kept her face steady by force of habit. "Did he leave a card?"

"No. He left a warning. He said the next fire wouldn't be about the building. It would be about the people who kept the building legal on paper." Brandt finally drank his coffee, grimaced, and stood. "I'll stay at my sister's in Riverside. You find out which of them — Voss, Ellery, or your ghost partner — is writing names in ash. And you do it before Nora Kim publishes something that paints a target on both of us."`,
