    chapter: "Chapter 2 — Names in Ash",
    text: `Nora Kim met Dana on a bench outside the Herald building with a recorder she promised, twice, to keep off unless Dana agreed. She was younger than her bylines suggested, sharp in a navy coat, and she listened to the ash-receipt story without the theatrical skepticism Dana had come to expect from reporters.

"I've been tracking Voss Properties' insurance pattern for eight months," Nora said. "Four fires in six years across two counties, all in buildings headed for redevelopment, all adjusted by Paige Ellery, all ruled accidental or undetermined with just enough ambiguity to pay out. Harbor Industrial was supposed to be the fifth. My sources said Voss needed the land clear before the waterfront bonding vote next quarter."

"That makes Voss the obvious suspect," Dana said.

"Obvious is not the same as true. Ellery's claim files show something else if you know where to look — restoration invoices from Meridian Chemical that don't match any actual remediation work I can verify on site. Money moving through a vendor that may not be doing the work it bills for. Someone is using fire claims as a laundry line." Nora opened her notebook to a page of dates. "Also, your former partner has been feeding me anonymous documents. I reverse-engineered the metadata on one PDF. It came from a machine registered to Cole Merritt's home address."

Dana felt the old bruise of that name press against her ribs. Cole had been her partner for six years, then her almost-husband for one disastrous winter, then a resignation letter after a propane-tank explosion she had ruled intentional and he had insisted was a tragic cascade failure. The review board sided with her. Cole left with his pension intact and his reputation in pieces.

"If Cole is feeding you documents," Dana said carefully, "he may be trying to steer the story toward Voss and Ellery while staying outside the blast radius."

"Or he may be the only person still willing to burn his own life down to expose them," Nora said. "I go to press in seventy-two hours unless you give me a reason to wait. Protect Theo Brandt. Find out who bought that specialty coating. And Dana — if the ash list is a schedule, I'm already late for my own funeral unless somebody stops the clock."`,
